{"id":"scatter_in_volume","compound_name":"Graphs::Scatter::scatter_in_volume","source_path":"scatter_in_volume/scatter_in_volume.json","category":"Scatter Pack","author":"Autodesk","description":"Simple scatter in volume","documentation_file":"scatter_in_volume_docs_ENU.md","documentation":"# `scatter_in_volume`\nGenerates a points object with points distributed inside a volume.  Positions, Colours and Geo_locations are also output. \n\n## Inputs\n\n### `input`\nVolume or mesh input for scattering into.  SDF and Fog volumes are supported, as well as mesh inputs.  The mesh is converted to a sdf volume internally.\n\n### `weights`\nWeights per point of the input geometry to control the distribution of generated points.\n* A weight of 0 or less means that no points are generated nearby.\n* A weight in the range 0—1 means that fewer than the normal number of points are generated nearby.\n* A weight of 1 or more means that the normal number of points are generated nearby.\n\nIf nothing is connected, the default weight is 1 for all points. This is an interpreted auto port that accepts the following types:\n* A single float uses the same weight for all points.\n* An array<float> uses the corresponding array element as the weight for each point. The array may be resized to match the number of points, if necessary.\n* A string uses the values of the property with that name as the weights. The property must exist on the geometry and consist of a float value per point.\n* A ScalarField uses the field's value at each point's position as the weight.\n* An array<long> specifies the indices of the points that have a weight of 1. All other points are considered to have a weight of 0.\n\n### `along_strand_profile`\nFCurve input controlling the profile of the points along the strand from the first point to the last\n\n### `around_strand_profile`\nFCurve input controlling the profile of the points out from the strnad from the min radius to the max radius\n\n\n## Scatter Settings\n### `scatter_mode`\nThe mode to use for scattering:\n* Random gives a uniform pseudo-random distribution. This is the closest to truly random, but exhibits clumps and bare patches that may be unwanted for some purposes.\n* BlueNoise gives a \"blue noise\" distribution. This is also referred to as a \"quasi-random\" or \"low discrepancy\" distribution. It provides a more even distribution, with points more equally spaced.\n* BlueNoiseMaximal gives a similar distribution to BlueNoise. This gives the most evenly-spaced distribution, but is the slowest to compute.\n\n### `amount_mode`\nControls the interpretation of the amount parameter: \n* Radius generates locations that are spaced at least 2 × amount units apart. If spheres with this radius are placed on the resulting points, none of the spheres would intersect. Points may be further apart, but never closer than twice this radius.\n* number generates the amount number of locations.\n* density generates amount locations per unit of surface area, strand length, or volume.\n\n### `amount`\nThe amount of points you wish to scatter around the strand.\n\n### `seed`\nThe seed for the random number generator.\n\n## Culling\n### `density_weights_mode`\nControls the interpretation of the density_weights parameter: \n* Random randomly deletes points. \n* DistanceBased deletes points that are too close to other points.\n\n### `cull_overlapping`\nRemoves points that are closer than 2 x overlapping_radius units to other points.\n\n### `overlapping_radius`\nControls the distance between points cull_overlapping is enabled.\n\n## Properties\n### `transfer_properties`\nA space-separated list of geo properties from the input geometry that are sampled and transferred to the output points. Only float, float2, float3, and float4 types are supported.\n### `set_emit_location`\nSets a property called point_emit_location on the output points.\n\n## Mesh To Volume\n### `General`\nThese (\"mtv_\") control the internal conversion from input mesh to level-set.\n\n### `mtv_volume_mode`\nDetermines how the input geometry is converted into a volume. Note that this mode only applies if the input geometry is a mesh.\n* In Solid mode the input mesh is voxelized with a solid interior.\n* In Shell mode the input mesh is voxelized as a thickened surface with a hollow interior.\n\n### `mtv_offset`\nThe world-space offset to apply to the output level set in world-space units. Positive values push the surface outwards, and negative values shrink it.\n\n### `mtv_min_hole_radius`\nFills gaps and holes that have a smaller radius than this value, when converting from meshes using Solid mode.\n\n### `mtv_volume_properties`\nA space-delimited list of properties to voxelize.\n\n## `Resolution`\n### `resolution_mode`\nThe resolution mode determines the units of detail_size.\n* In Automatic mode, detail_size is interpreted in world-space units if geometry is points (i.e. like Absolute mode); otherwise detail_size is interpreted as relative to the bounding box of the input geometry (i.e. like Relative mode).\n* In Absolute mode detail_size is specified in world-space units.\n* In Relative mode detail_size is specified relative to the bounding box of the input geometry.\n\n### `mtv_detail_size`\nSpecifies the world-space size of the voxels in the output volume.\n\n### `mtv_adaptivity`\nDetermines the type of spatial adaptivity used in the conversion.\n* Off: No adaptivity is used.\n* Automatic: A form of automatic adaptivity where planar and smooth regions are voxelized more coarsely. Note that when the bandwidth is enabled, the voxels inside the bandwidth remain fine.\n* VariedFromProperty: The voxel-size of the output volume can be varied in space by supplying a custom property as specified by adaptivity_property below. This gives you complete freedom to choose where you want detail.\n\n### `mtv_adaptivity property`\nThe name of a float property containing the absolute detail size used in VariedFromProperty mode.\n\n### `enable bandwidth`\nIf enabled, guarantees high-resolution voxels in a band of bandwidth voxel-space units around the input geometry. This is useful if you plan to e.g. apply large displacements to the level set surface and you are experiencing artifacts due the surface being displaced to coarser voxels.\n\n### `mtv_bandwidth`\nThe world-space bandwidth to guarantee around the input geometry.\n\n\n## Display\n### `point_color`\nThe point colour at the surface of the volume.\n\n### `point_shape`\nHow the points will be visualized, in what shape.\n\n### `default_point_size`\nThe size on screen to visualize the points.\n\n### `depth`\nHow far into the volume the point colour extends.\n\n### `depth curve`\nA curve controlling how the depth value is interpreted.\n\n## Outputs\n### `points`\nThe resulting points object.\n\n### `positions`\nThe positions of the resulting points.\n\n### `colors`\nThe colors of the resulting points.\n\n### `geo_locations`\nThe locations of the resulting points.","library_dependencies":{"Amino":"2.15.0.9","bif":"2.15.0.9","fields":"2.15.0.9","geometries":"2.15.0.9","scatter":"2.15.0.9"},"graph_ports":[],"terminal_states":[{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::final","enabled":"true"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::diagnostic","enabled":"true"}],"counts":{"nodes":52,"connections":81,"values":70,"compounds":8},"node_types":{"Value::Math::float3":5,"Core::Logic::if":3,"Geometry::Properties::set_geo_property":3,"Core::Graph::pass":3,"Geometry::Query::sample_volume":2,"Value::string":2,"Rendering::Materials::set_geo_property_reference":2,"Modeling::Primitive::create_mesh_sphere":1,"Geometry::Converters::mesh_to_volume":1,"Modeling::Points::scatter_points":1,"Geometry::Common::get_geo_schema_type":1,"Modeling::Points::cull_points":1,"Core::Fields::fractal_noise_field":1,"point_display":1,"Diagnostic::Display::point_scope":1,"Core::Graph::terminal":1,"Geometry::Properties::get_point_position":1,"Core::FCurve::evaluate_fcurve":1,"Core::Math::divide":1,"Core::Array: